(WARNING: grammar spoilers ahead...)


(3,4,5,7,9,12,13,14,15,17,19) One correct answer each.

(1)  One correct answer, though grammar evolutionists would say
"snuck" has slowly evolved into the past of "sneak".

(2)  No correct answer.  Succinctness counts, and "The puppy without a
home..." has the same meaning and none of the grammatical questioning.

(6)  The correct answer depends on the context whence the sentence
came.  If Chrissy's teacher is *in* Room 22, then "bring" is correct;
otherwise "take" is correct.

(8)  "Everybody was satisfied with their grades."  Note the final "s".

(10) Even grammar nazis like me no longer use the word "shall" unless
we're trying to be archaic or pedantic.

(11) Yes, you shouldn't normally split infinitives, but in this
particularly oft-ridiculed case, the cadence of the sentence becomes
more important than strict textbook grammar.

(16) No correct answer.  Rewrite the damned sentence to eliminate the
problem.  -- He asked her what she thought about "No new taxes."

(18) I'll be the first to admit that I may not like language
evolution, but I'll still admit that no one who speaks English uses
the subjunctive case any longer outside a small group of
pedants--which group includes myself.  But then I've already used
'whence' above, so what does that say?

(20) No correct answer under normal use.  Write "Mr. Jones and Mr.
Smith".

